THINK = {
1: r"Think of a judge as a very strict, very fast friend who only ever says ``yes'' or ``no.'' It cannot see your effort, only your output. So the real skill is not typing quickly; it is predicting, before you write anything, whether your idea will be fast enough and right enough. Ask two questions every time: \emph{How big can the input get?} and \emph{How many steps does my idea take on that input?} If the second number is much bigger than a hundred million, change the idea, not the code.",
2: r"When you do not know a clever method, there is always a humble one: try everything. Complete search is the safety net under every other technique in this book, and often it is already fast enough. The way of thinking is: first picture \emph{all} the possibilities as a tree of choices, then ask which branches you can cut off early because they cannot possibly lead to an answer.",
3: r"A greedy algorithm is a bet that the locally best choice is also globally safe. Bets can be wrong, so never ask ``does this feel right?'' Ask ``if some perfect answer exists, can I swap my choice into it without making it worse?'' If yes, the bet is safe. If you cannot find that swap, look for a tiny counterexample instead; one is usually enough.",
4: r"Binary search is not really about sorted arrays. It is about any question whose answer flips from ``no'' to ``yes'' exactly once as you walk along a line, like ``is a speed of $x$ enough to arrive on time?'' If you can ask such a question, you can find the flip point in about $\log_2 n$ steps by repeatedly halving the possibilities. Your job in a problem is to \emph{discover the question}; the code is a fixed recipe.",
5: r"If you keep recomputing the same thing, store it. A prefix sum is the simplest case: spend one pass remembering running totals, and every later question becomes a subtraction. Whenever a problem repeats ``over a range'' or ``over a window,'' ask: \emph{what small summary could I build once so that each query needs only a glance?}",
6: r"Some loops look slow because one loop sits inside another, but when you count the \emph{total} work across the whole run, each element is touched a fixed number of times. That is amortised thinking: judge the cost of the whole process, not of one step. The structures here all keep a tidy ``waiting list'' of candidates, and throw away anything that can never be the answer again.",
7: r"A graph is simply a way to draw ``who is connected to whom.'' Many problems that do not mention graphs at all (a puzzle, a maze, a set of prerequisites) become easy once you decide what the dots and the lines are. The first question to ask is always: \emph{what is a vertex, and what is an edge?} Everything else is a walk through the picture.",
8: r"A shortest path is built from small improvements: if going through $u$ makes $v$ closer, update $v$. All the algorithms here are different orders for doing the same ``relaxation,'' and the order is chosen to match what the edge weights allow. Match the tool to the graph: no weights, use BFS; non-negative weights, use Dijkstra; negative weights, use Bellman--Ford.",
9: r"Imagine people at a party forming groups: when two people discover a mutual friend, their groups merge, and you never need to split a group again. That one-way merging is what makes the disjoint-set structure so cheap. For spanning trees, the thinking is: to connect everything as cheaply as possible, keep adding the cheapest edge that does not close a loop.",
10: r"A tree is a graph with no loops, so there is exactly one way to get anywhere. That removes most of the difficulty: you can solve a problem for a small subtree and combine the answers upward. The habit to build is ``solve my children first, then combine,'' which is a recursive way of thinking that returns again in dynamic programming.",
11: r"Dynamic programming sounds grand but is a modest idea: write the slow recursive solution, notice that it keeps solving the same small problems, and store each answer the first time. The skill is choosing the \emph{state}: the smallest description of where you are that still tells you everything you need about the future. Always say your state and your transition in one plain sentence before you write a line of code.",
12: r"The previous chapter's idea is the same; only the \emph{state} gets fancier. When there are few items, a state can be a set (a bitmask). When digits matter, it can be a position and a bound. When the problem is about a stretch of an array, it can be a pair of endpoints. If you are stuck, ask: \emph{what do I need to remember so that the rest of the problem does not care how I got here?}",
13: r"If the data never changes, a prefix sum is enough. When it does change, you need a structure that updates and answers quickly, and the trick is to store summaries of \emph{blocks of sizes that are powers of two}, so that any range is a few blocks and any update touches a few blocks. Think of it as an index at the back of a book that has to be re-written cheaply when a page changes.",
14: r"Number theory in contests is mostly about two habits: reduce numbers early (mod $p$) so they never grow too large, and trust a few well-tested identities rather than rediscovering them. When a formula asks for division under a modulus, remember that ``divide'' really means ``multiply by an inverse,'' and the inverse exists for the reason Fermat's theorem gives.",
15: r"Comparing two strings character by character is slow if you do it many times. The ideas here all squeeze a string into something smaller that you can compare quickly: a hash is a fingerprint, the prefix function remembers how much of the pattern you have already matched, and a trie shares common beginnings. Always ask what information about the pattern you can reuse instead of throwing it away.",
16: r"Geometry looks messy because of decimals. The way of thinking here is to avoid them: express every question as the sign of a whole-number expression, such as ``does this turn left?''. Then the computer never has to decide whether $0.1+0.2$ equals $0.3$. If you do all your tests with exact integers, the answers are exactly right.",
17: r"Do not memorise probability formulas. Picture the experiment as a list of equally likely outcomes and count the ones you want. When counting directly is painful, switch viewpoints: expected values add up even when events are tangled together, so break the quantity into small indicator pieces and add their expectations.",
18: r"Strong contestants are not the ones who know the most algorithms; they are the ones who waste the least time. The way of thinking is to treat a contest as a project: read everything first, solve the sure things, test before submitting, and learn from each mistake afterwards. After the contest, solving the problems you could not do is where most of the improvement happens.",
}
